          {
            "roundId": 1,
            "reply": "GitHub provides email notification options for various events, but not for \"every thing\" automatically. Here\u0027s a summary based on GitHub\u0027s features:\n\n### Repository-Level Email Notifications\nIn your repository settings (go to **Settings** \u003e **Notifications**), you can configure email alerts for:\n- **Issues**: Opened, closed, reopened, comments, etc.\n- **Pull Requests**: Opened, closed, merged, comments, reviews, etc.\n- **Releases**: New releases.\n- **Security Alerts**: Code scanning alerts (from CodeQL), dependabot alerts, secret scanning.\n- **Discussions** (if enabled).\n- **Pushes** (to watched branches).\n- **Forks** and other repository changes.\n\nYou can choose to receive emails for \"Participating\" (only when you\u0027re mentioned or participating), \"All activity\", or \"Only mentions\". You can also customize per event type.\n\n### Turning Off Emails\nTo disable emails for specific events (like PR conversations or CodeQL alerts):\n1. Go to your repository\u0027s **Settings** \u003e **Notifications**.\n2. Uncheck the \"Email\" box for the events you want to disable (e.g., Pull requests, Security alerts).\n3. For CodeQL/security alerts specifically, go to **Settings** \u003e **Security** \u003e **Code scanning** and uncheck \"Email alerts\".\n\n### User-Level Settings\nIn your personal GitHub settings (**Settings** \u003e **Notifications**), you can globally disable email notifications or customize them further.\n\n### GitHub Actions and Emails\nGitHub Actions workflows don\u0027t send emails by default, but you can add steps to send custom emails using third-party actions like `dawidd6/action-send-mail` if needed. For example, to notify on workflow failures.\n\nIf you want to modify your workflows to avoid triggering notifications (e.g., by not running on PRs or not uploading CodeQL results), let me know the specific changes. For instance, I can update the workflows to remove PR triggers or add conditions.",
